    "answer": {
      "short": "The result is odd.\n\nThe sum of the conjugate powers is an even integer, and $0<(2-\\sqrt{3})^n<1$, so $(2+\\sqrt{3})^n$ lies immediately below that even integer.",
      "worked": "Let $S=(2+\\sqrt{3})^n+(2-\\sqrt{3})^n$. In the two binomial expansions, terms with odd powers of $\\sqrt{3}$ cancel. Therefore\n$$S=2\\sum_{j=0}^{\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor}\\binom{n}{2j}2^{n-2j}3^j,$$\nwhich is an even integer. Since $0<2-\\sqrt{3}<1$ and $n\\geq1$,\n$$0<(2-\\sqrt{3})^n<1.$$\nHence\n$$S-1<(2+\\sqrt{3})^n<S.$$\nRounding down gives $S-1$, which is odd.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
